---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import MetaslabLab from "../../components/MetaslabLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("metaslabs");
const M = "module/zfs/metaslab.c";
const SMC = "module/zfs/space_map.c";
const SMH = "include/sys/space_map.h";
const L = {
  struct: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 339),
  trees: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 375, 388),
  lifecycle: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 285, 338),
  rollback: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 295, 302),
  deferSize: gh("include/sys/txg.h", 49),
  setSize: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 3021, 3026),
  sizeTable: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 3006, 3013),
  msCount: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 91),
  shift: gh(M, 2920, 2921),
  lbas: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 506),
  fallback: gh(M, 1933, 1954),
  threshold: gh(M, 169),
  freePct: gh(M, 177),
  maxSearch: gh(M, 192),
  minSearch: gh(M, 199),
  picker: gh(M, 1771, 1802),
  merge: gh("module/zfs/range_tree.c", 387, 394),
  freeing: gh(M, 5518, 5528),
  swap: gh(M, 4493, 4494),
  early: gh(M, 4279, 4284),
  intro: gh(SMC, 529, 543),
  emptyTree: gh(SMC, 775, 778),
  smpAlloc: gh(SMC, 780, 783),
  feature: gh("module/zcommon/zfeature_common.c", 427, 431),
  sentinel: gh(SMH, 163),
  macros: gh(SMH, 175, 203),
  bf64: gh("include/sys/bitops.h", 46, 48),
  pad: gh(SMC, 615, 631),
  kinds: gh(SMC, 63, 80),
  iterate: gh(SMC, 121, 142),
  loadLength: gh(SMC, 423, 443),
  syncedLength: gh(M, 2514),
  loadDefer: gh(M, 2649, 2652),
  neverWritten: gh(M, 2543, 2557),
  stayDirty: gh(M, 4645, 4651),
  slop: gh(M, 4573, 4578),
  condenseCall: gh(M, 4363, 4365),
  condensePct: gh(M, 89),
  blockThreshold: gh(M, 104),
  blksz: gh(M, 74),
  blkszLog: gh(M, 81),
  estimate: gh(SMC, 1033, 1085),
  histogram: gh("module/zfs/range_tree.c", 131, 132),
  condenseTree: gh(M, 3949, 3957),
  condensing: gh(M, 3974),
  truncate: gh(SMC, 908, 910),
  rotor: gh(M, 5431, 5464),
  weight: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 469),
  logSm: gh(M, 4373, 4396),
  unflushed: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 521, 522),
  logFeature: gh("module/zcommon/zfeature_common.c", 481, 491),
  createAll: gh("cmd/zpool/zpool_main.c", 1959, 1961),
  logDesign: gh("module/zfs/spa_log_spacemap.c", 36, 52),
  blkszWhy: gh(M, 66, 81),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="metaslabs"
  title="Metaslabs and space maps · Uberblok"
  description="How OpenZFS 2.4.4 finds free space: vdevs cut into metaslabs, a free tree in memory, and an append-only space map of ALLOC and FREE entries, condensed when it grows too long. Interactive, with the code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          Copy-on-write never overwrites a block: every write allocates new space and every overwrite frees the old block.
          Allocation is on the path of every write, so it has to be fast, and what is free has to be recorded on disk, so
          that the pool still knows it after a reboot.
        </p>
        <p>
          The obvious record is a bitmap of the whole pool, one bit per sector. A pool of 16 TiB with 4 KiB sectors has
          2<sup>32</sup> of them: 512 MiB of bitmap to keep in memory, and it would have to be updated in place on every change, which is exactly what
          copy-on-write forbids. ZFS does something else. It cuts each vdev into a few hundred <em>metaslabs</em>, keeps the
          free space of a metaslab as a tree of free segments in memory, and records on disk only what changed, as a
          <em>space map</em>: a log of ALLOC and FREE extents that is only ever appended to, and rewritten short when it has
          grown too long.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Write, delete, sync</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Below, a vdev of 8 GiB with 4 KiB sectors, cut into 16 metaslabs of 512 MiB; every write goes to metaslab 2,
          drawn underneath. Two files are already there: A, 32 MiB, written in txg 10, and B, 4 MiB, in txg 11. The tape
          is the metaslab’s space map, 64-bit words on disk; select an entry to see its bits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Delete A and sync: a FREE entry joins the tape, but A’s space is deferred, not free, for two more txgs. Sync twice
          to get it back, then write 160 MiB: dynamic fit does not go back to A’s hole, and a run that long needs a two-word
          entry. Press Load to rebuild the free tree from the tape, and Condense: it is refused while the map is short.
          Rewrite the database until the test says yes, then condense.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MetaslabLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          ZFS keeps free space per metaslab, as a tree in memory and an append-only log on disk that, replayed, rebuilds the
          tree, and it rewrites the log from the tree when the log has grown much longer than the tree.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A top-level vdev is cut into metaslabs, about 200 by default, from 512 MiB to 16 GiB each (fewer and smaller on small vdevs). A loaded metaslab
          has its free segments in a range tree, <code>ms_allocatable</code>. An allocation takes a segment from that tree,
          by default with the dynamic-fit allocator, which searches forward from where the last allocation of that size
          ended, and puts it in a per-txg tree, <code>ms_allocating</code>; a free goes to <code>ms_freeing</code>.
          Nothing reaches the disk until the txg syncs: then the txg’s allocations and frees are appended to the
          metaslab’s space map, a debug word and ALLOC entries, a debug word and FREE entries, one or two 64-bit words per
          extent. Freed space becomes allocatable again only two txgs later, so that the pool can be rolled back to an
          older uberblock without finding its blocks reused. Loading a metaslab replays its map, adding FREE and removing
          ALLOC extents. And when the map is at least twice as long as the free tree would be written alone, and longer
          than four blocks, the next sync condenses it if the metaslab is loaded: the map is truncated and rewritten from the tree.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Metaslabs.</strong> A metaslab, <a href={L.struct}><code>struct metaslab</code></a>, is a range of
            a vdev with <a href={L.trees}>its in-core trees</a>: <code>ms_allocatable</code>, the free tree,
            <code>ms_allocating[]</code>, one per txg in flight, <code>ms_freeing</code>, <code>ms_freed</code> and
            <code>ms_defer[]</code>; a comment in the header <a href={L.lifecycle}>draws their life cycle</a>. Their size
            comes from <a href={L.setSize}><code>vdev_metaslab_set_size</code></a>: it aims at
            <a href={L.msCount}>200 metaslabs</a>, within 512 MiB and 16 GiB, and <a href={L.sizeTable}>at least 16</a>
            on a small vdev. The figure’s vdev, 8 GiB, gets 16 metaslabs of 512 MiB. A metaslab’s map counts in sectors:
            <a href={L.shift}>its shift is the vdev’s <code>ashift</code></a>, so a unit is 4 KiB here.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Taking a block.</strong> Allocating from a metaslab:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={M}
          from={4863}
          to={4890}
          symbol="metaslab_block_alloc"
          notes={[
            { from: 4864, text: "No allocation from a metaslab whose map is being condensed: its free tree is being written out." },
            {
              from: 4868,
              text: "The class’s allocator, dynamic fit by default, returns an offset in this metaslab.",
            },
            { from: 4877, text: "The extent leaves the free tree." },
            {
              from: 4880,
              to: 4881,
              text: "The first allocation of a txg marks the metaslab dirty, so the sync will visit it.",
            },
            {
              from: 4883,
              to: 4884,
              text: "The extent goes into <code>ms_allocating</code> for this txg. Nothing is written to disk yet. In the figure, the orange extents.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>Dynamic fit.</strong> Which offset the allocator returns:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={M}
          from={1905}
          to={1931}
          symbol="metaslab_df_alloc"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1905,
              to: 1906,
              text: `One cursor per power-of-two alignment, <a href="${L.lbas}"><code>ms_lbas[]</code></a>. Every block in the figure is 128 KiB, so one cursor, <code>ms_lbas[17]</code>, the black mark above the metaslab.`,
            },
            {
              from: 1917,
              to: 1921,
              text: `Under <a href="${L.freePct}">4% free</a>, or when no free segment is as large as <a href="${L.threshold}">128 KiB</a>, no forward search.`,
            },
            {
              from: 1923,
              to: 1924,
              text: `Otherwise <a href="${L.picker}"><code>metaslab_block_picker</code></a>: the first free segment at or after the cursor that fits, searched at most <a href="${L.maxSearch}">16 MiB</a> past the first candidate, and always at least <a href="${L.minSearch}">100 segments</a>; the cursor moves to the end of what was taken.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            If that finds nothing, <a href={L.fallback}>the fallback</a> takes, from a tree of the same free segments sorted
            by size, a segment of exactly the size asked or the next larger one, and moves the cursor there. So space freed
            behind the cursor is not reused until the cursor reaches the end of the metaslab, or the metaslab runs low: in
            the figure, the 160 MiB file lands after B, not in A’s hole. Blocks taken one after the other are adjacent, and
            the range tree <a href={L.merge}>merges adjacent segments</a>, so a file written in one txg is one extent, and one
            entry on the map.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Freeing.</strong> A free <a href={L.freeing}>goes to <code>ms_freeing</code></a>, in syncing
            context. The block stays allocated until the sync is over.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Syncing: appending to the map.</strong> In <code>metaslab_sync</code>:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={M}
          from={4367}
          to={4405}
          symbol="metaslab_sync"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4373,
              to: 4396,
              text: "With a log space map, the txg’s allocations and frees go to one pool-wide log instead, with the vdev’s id. See below; the figure takes the other branch.",
            },
            {
              from: 4400,
              to: 4403,
              text: "The txg’s allocation tree is appended as ALLOC entries, then <code>ms_freeing</code> as FREE entries, to the metaslab’s own map, without a vdev id. Nothing already on disk is rewritten.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Each append <a href={L.intro}>starts with a debug word</a>, which records the txg and the sync pass, and an
            empty tree <a href={L.emptyTree}>appends nothing</a>; ALLOC adds to the map’s allocated total,
            <code>smp_alloc</code>, and FREE subtracts from it (<a href={L.smpAlloc}>L780–L783</a>). A metaslab without
            any allocation or free in a txg <a href={L.early}>is left alone</a>. Then <a href={L.swap}><code>ms_freeing</code>
            becomes <code>ms_freed</code></a>. How many words an extent takes:
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SMC}
          from={704}
          to={739}
          symbol="space_map_write_impl"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 708,
              to: 711,
              text: "Offset and length in units of 4 KiB, from the start of the metaslab.",
            },
            {
              from: 728,
              to: 734,
              text: `Two words when the offset does not fit in 47 bits, the run is longer than <code>SM_RUN_MAX</code>, 2<sup>15</sup> units (128 MiB here), or a vdev id must be stored, and the <code>spacemap_v2</code> feature is active. It is <a href="${L.feature}">activated as soon as it is enabled</a>, which a new pool does.`,
            },
            { from: 732, to: 733, text: "A test tunable, off by default." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>The words.</strong> The layout, as the header draws it:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SMH}
          from={104}
          to={140}
          symbol="space map entries"
          notes={[
            { from: 104, to: 111, text: "A debug word: prefix 10, the action (0 ALLOC, 1 FREE), the sync pass, the txg." },
            {
              from: 114,
              to: 120,
              text: "A one-word entry: top bit 0, a 47-bit offset, a type bit and a 15-bit run. The run is stored minus one, so it goes from 1 to 2<sup>15</sup>.",
            },
            {
              from: 123,
              to: 135,
              text: "A two-word entry: prefix 11, a 36-bit run and a 24-bit vdev id, then the type and a 63-bit offset.",
            },
            {
              from: 137,
              to: 139,
              text: "In the figure, a pad word appears when a two-word entry would start on the last word of a 16 KiB block.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The three kinds are told apart <a href={L.kinds}>by their top bits</a>. The figure encodes every word with
            <a href={L.macros}>the header’s macros</a>, <code>BF64_ENCODE</code> <a href={L.bf64}>masking then shifting</a>:
            A’s ALLOC entry, offset 0 and 8192 units, is <code>0x0000000000001FFF</code>; the 160 MiB extent, offset 0x2400
            and 40960 units, is <code>0xC000009FFF000000</code> then <code>0x0000000000002400</code>. The vdev field of
            that first word is 0: <a href={L.sentinel}><code>SM_NO_VDEVID</code></a> is <code>1 &lt;&lt; 24</code>, and the
            24-bit mask drops it. The words are written here:
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SMC}
          from={633}
          to={663}
          symbol="space_map_write_seg"
          notes={[
            { from: 633, text: "A run longer than the format allows is cut; with two-word entries that is 2<sup>36</sup> units, never reached in a metaslab." },
            { from: 636, to: 638, text: "One word: offset, type, run." },
            { from: 643, to: 651, text: "Two words: prefix, run and vdev; then type and offset." },
            { from: 659, text: "The map grows by one or two words. It only ever grows, until it is condensed." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Before a two-word entry, <a href={L.pad}>the last word of a block is padded</a> with an empty debug word, which
            loading <a href={L.iterate}>skips</a>.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Two txgs of grace.</strong> At the end of each txg’s sync:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={M}
          from={4624}
          to={4638}
          symbol="metaslab_sync_done"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4630,
              to: 4631,
              text: "The frees deferred two txgs ago go back into the free tree: <code>defer_tree</code> is <code>ms_defer[txg % TXG_DEFER_SIZE]</code>.",
            },
            { from: 4632, to: 4633, text: "This txg’s frees take their place." },
            {
              from: 4634,
              to: 4637,
              text: `Not deferred only when the pool is <a href="${L.slop}">almost full, or a vdev is being removed or expanded</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <a href={L.deferSize}><code>TXG_DEFER_SIZE</code> is 2</a>: a block freed in txg T is on the map as FREE after the
            sync of T, but allocatable only after the sync of T + 2. The header says why: <a href={L.rollback}>“a safety net
            for uberblock rollback”</a>; the pool can be rolled back two txgs and find none of its blocks reused. A metaslab
            with deferred space <a href={L.stayDirty}>stays dirty</a> until the frees are back.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Loading: replaying the log.</strong> Each entry of the map, in order:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SMC}
          from={401}
          to={416}
          symbol="space_map_load_callback"
          notes={[
            { from: 405, to: 409, text: "Loading free space: a FREE entry is added to the tree." },
            { from: 410, to: 413, text: "An ALLOC entry is removed from it." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The tree <a href={L.loadLength}>starts as the whole metaslab</a>, so replaying the log from the start leaves
            exactly the free space. <code>metaslab_load</code> replays up to <a href={L.syncedLength}>the length at the last
            sync</a> and then <a href={L.loadDefer}>removes the deferred trees</a>, which are FREE on the map but not
            allocatable yet. A metaslab whose map was never written <a href={L.neverWritten}>loads as free</a>. The figure’s
            Load does exactly this and compares the result with the tree it had.
          </p>
          <p><strong>When to condense.</strong> At each sync, in its first pass, if the metaslab is loaded, <code>metaslab_sync</code> <a href={L.condenseCall}>asks</a>:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={M}
          from={3835}
          to={3861}
          symbol="metaslab_should_condense"
          notes={[
            { from: 3851, to: 3853, text: "An empty free tree, or a requested condense: always." },
            { from: 3855, text: "A record is the larger of the map’s block size and a sector." },
            {
              from: 3857,
              to: 3858,
              text: `The optimal size: an estimate of the free tree written alone, from <a href="${L.histogram}">its histogram of segment sizes</a>, one word per segment under 128 MiB, two above, plus padding (<a href="${L.estimate}">space_map.c L1033–L1085</a>).`,
            },
            {
              from: 3860,
              to: 3861,
              text: `Condense when the map is at least <a href="${L.condensePct}">200%</a> of that, and longer than <a href="${L.blockThreshold}">4 records</a>. Without a log space map, <a href="${L.blksz}">a block is 16 KiB</a>: 64 KiB. In the figure, the two tests under the tape.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            In the figure, A, B and a deleted C make a map of 96 bytes against an optimal 32: twice as long, but far from
            64 KiB, so no. The rewrites of a 64 MiB database, 128 random blocks per txg, append a FREE entry for almost every
            old block; after about 70 txgs the map passes 64 KiB, while the free tree, about two hundred segments, would
            take under 2 KiB.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Condensing.</strong> The map is rewritten from the tree:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={M}
          from={3994}
          to={4013}
          symbol="metaslab_condense"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 3994,
              to: 4003,
              text: "This txg’s own entries are still appended after the condensed map, as usual.",
            },
            { from: 4009, to: 4010, text: "One ALLOC entry for the whole metaslab: 2<sup>17</sup> units, a two-word entry." },
            { from: 4011, text: "One FREE entry per free segment." },
            {
              from: 4012,
              text: `Then the condense tree: <a href="${L.condenseTree}">the deferred frees and the allocations of the txgs in flight</a>, which are free as far as the old map knows.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Before writing, the metaslab is <a href={L.condensing}>marked as condensing</a>, so nothing is allocated from it
            meanwhile, and the map is <a href={L.truncate}>truncated to length 0</a>. Replaying the short map gives the same
            tree as replaying the long one: the figure’s Load, after a condense, says so.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="logsm">
      <h2 id="logsm">Coda: the log space map</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>zpool create</code> <a href={L.createAll}>enables every feature</a> unless told otherwise, among them
          <a href={L.logFeature}><code>log_spacemap</code></a>, and then <code>metaslab_sync</code> takes
          <a href={L.logSm}>the other branch</a>: each txg’s allocations and frees, for every metaslab, are appended to one
          log for the whole pool, as two-word entries that carry the vdev’s id, and each metaslab keeps them in memory as
          <a href={L.unflushed}>unflushed changes</a>. Every txg, <a href={L.logDesign}>a selected group of metaslabs is
          flushed</a>: their changes are written to their own maps, the same entries as in the figure, in blocks of
          <a href={L.blkszLog}>128 KiB</a> rather than 16 KiB. The design comment names the workload it is for: random writes,
          which free a little everywhere and would otherwise <a href={L.blkszWhy}>touch many metaslabs’ maps every txg</a>.
          The figure shows a metaslab’s own map, written at every sync, because that is where the idea is: the log delays
          the same entries and gathers them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the functions above on one metaslab of one vdev, with 4 KiB sectors and no log space map. Every block
        is 128 KiB, so one cursor; there is one txg in flight, synced at once in one pass, without quiescing, so
        <code>ms_allocating</code> has one tree in use. Which metaslab an allocation goes to, by
        <a href={L.weight}>weight</a> and through <a href={L.rotor}>the rotor over vdevs</a>, is left out: everything goes
        to metaslab 2, and a write that does not fit is refused instead of moving on. So are allocation throttling, gang
        blocks, ditto copies, checkpoints, trim and the space map’s histogram. Condensing follows
        <code>metaslab_should_condense</code>, at the sync that writes to the map, as <code>metaslab_sync</code> does; the
        rewrites stop just before such a sync, so it can be watched. Load is shown on a txg with nothing pending. The rest of
        the visit is in <a href={roomUrl(lang, "txg")}>Transaction groups</a> for when a txg syncs, and in
        <a href={roomUrl(lang, "snapshots")}>Snapshots and clones</a> for when a block is freed at all.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="metaslabs" />
</Base>

